The formals check is a definition: draco.FormalsRefusalOf returns the reasons, read by the emitter, the parser and the Scala generator (GitHub #11)

OWNING ISSUE: GitHub #11 (open; stays open). Nothing on the tracker was touched.

WHY. After 980de86 one refusal was still hand-written on the emit side: `MethodTypeParameters.validate`, 30 lines in mods,
called by the drake emitter (`refuseElement`), the drake parser (`checkedDynamic`) and the Scala generator (`methodName`).
What it checks is a property of the definition, not of a target: a method's name carries no formals, no formal is declared
twice, each formal is a name, each bound is a well-formed type form carrying no bound marker. Dev accepted "Accepted, build
it" on a definition in the core `draco` domain, beside TypeParameter, read by all three callers.

WHAT.
- New trio `draco.FormalsRefusalOf`: factory `par element TypeElement`, one element `reasons [Text]`, with the messages the
  hand-written check raised, in its order: the name, the duplicates, then per formal its name, its lower bound, its upper
  bound. Only a method (an element whose kind definition is Dynamic) is judged. Two globals carry the recursion over a
  bound: `wellFormed` (a non-blank text leaf, or a single-key node of the right arity — `[]` `[]+` one operand, `{}` `{}+`
  one or two, `->` two, `()` `(,)` two or more — whose operands are well formed) and `leaves` (a form's text leaves in
  order). Authored in drake with its `uses` record, JSON `DrakeCLI parse`, Scala `GeneratorCLI generate`; listed by Draco.
- `gendrake.ElementRefusalOf`: `problems` is the keyword problem, one Problem per formals reason, one per value reason, the
  block-value problem, then the recursion — the order the hand-written emitter judged them in.
- Drake.scala: `refuseElement` and the hand walk in `refuseDefinition` are removed, which is now the one line raising the
  first of `RefusalOf(td).elementProblems`; the parser's `checkedDynamic` raises the first reason. DracoGenerator's
  `methodName` raises the first reason. `MethodTypeParameters.scala` is deleted.
- The §6 generation order was followed (FormalsRefusalOf generated and compiled, then ElementRefusalOf and Draco); the
  three trios are stable on the recompiled engine.
- FormalsRefusalOfTest (7), the 95th suite: well-formed methods and non-methods; each reason with its message; every
  reason in order; the arity rows and the leaves; the element read once and lazily; the emitter, the parser and the Scala
  generator each raising the first reason; no method of the corpus refused (104 methods, 10 carrying formals).
  MethodTypeParameterTest's two direct calls and ElementRefusalOfTest's emitter test restated; no test removed.

DIFFERENCES. (1) As recommended: the messages lose the host prefix "requirement failed: "; every reason is reported, the
first unchanged; on the emit side the formals are judged in element order with the other refusals, where they were judged
after them over the whole definition. (2) NOT as recommended: a bound marker (`<:`, `>:`, `: `) is reported per text leaf,
"Invalid bound for 'T': <leaf>", where the hand-written check printed the whole bound's text. For a bound that is one leaf
— the only shape the parser can produce a marker in — the message is identical. Spelling the whole bound would have made
core read `gendrake.TypeFormOf`. One edge follows: a leaf ending in a colon inside a tree, which the old text check caught
through the punctuation after it, is not reported.

PROBED IN BUILDING. The bound recursion, unprobed at the recommendation, generated and compiled as written (two recursive
globals, the nine-key dispatch not needed: the node's key is read through `Dracon.keys`). Quoted brackets and parentheses
in values parse as written.

